From 13b70494d51c5ff6c31475c3813ba7fa5a727479 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 23:12:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Material managers: one layout per array

The layout of a material manager was a single FieldLayout. It becomes a
layout per array: MaterialStateManagerLayout gives the layouts of the
gradients, of the thermodynamic forces, of the internal state variables
and of the values given at each point for the external state variables
with several components. MaterialDataManagerLayout adds the layout of
the tangent operator blocks. Both structures are implicitly built from
a FieldLayout, which gives the same layout to all the arrays, and are
compared by ==: initializer.layout = FieldLayout::X and the comparisons
of the layout member with a FieldLayout keep compiling.
getStateLayout gives the layout of the states of a material data
manager, getUniformLayout the layout of all the arrays if they have the
same one.

Every site uses the layout of the array it handles: views on the
arrays and on the variables, views of make_view, duplication of the
uniform external state variables, HDF5 files, extraction of the
internal state variables, finite strain conversions (layout of the
thermodynamic forces for the stress, of the tangent operator blocks for
the tangent operator). The inputs of the initialize functions and the
outputs of the post-processings given by a std::span are stored in the
layout of the manager, which must then be the same for all its arrays:
otherwise, an error tells to give them by a FieldView. The views of the
material function managers are built from the views of the groups of
variables. The integration point by point gathers the components which
are not contiguous, and the integration of batches reads the strides of
the views: both handle any layout per array.

C bindings: the get_layout functions report an error if the layouts
differ. Python bindings: both layout structures are exposed.
